TASK
Write the 2-3 recurring procedures this module is actually run by — the routines a staff member or the owner would follow (e.g. for a booking module: "Handling a new booking request", "The daily schedule check", "When a double-booking happens"). Choose what THIS module genuinely repeats; 2 is fine.

Rules per procedure:
- phase — every procedure declares when it becomes runnable:
  "current"  = staff can run it TODAY with what already exists (their phones, their spreadsheets, their habits);
  "pilot"    = runs during the recommended pilot, with the pilot's minimal tooling;
  "future"   = depends on a module that must be BUILT first.
  Never present an unbuilt screen, portal, dashboard or automation as something staff can use today — a procedure whose steps need the new system is "future", full stop.
- THRESHOLDS: any waiting period, cut-off time, retry count, operating-hours window, SLA or monitoring interval must be either a fact the client stated, or written as "<value> (proposed — client approval required)". Never an unlabeled invented number.
- ONE CONDITION, ONE NUMBER: the same event may never carry two different waits or retry counts anywhere in your output — a customer flagged unreachable "after 15 minutes" in a step and "after 30 minutes" in an exception is a contradiction, not a nuance. Pick one value and reuse it.
- Name systems by their EXACT module name from the module spec, or by a tool the client said they already use. One capability, one name — no synonyms, no rebrands.
- trigger: the concrete event or time that starts it.
- 4-8 numbered steps. One actor per step. actor is "ai" (name this module or its agent), "you", or a real staff role. Steps the AI executes alone say so — that is the product.
- Steps are checkable actions ("Confirm the deposit shows in the day sheet"), never descriptions ("The system manages payments").
- exceptions: 1-3 real deviations and what to do — the "when it goes wrong" a manual always covers.
- Everything traces to this module's spec or stated facts. No invented tools, prices, or vendors.


